<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kakongo</h1><p class="st-meta">1550 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Kinguele</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Kongo coastal kingdom between Loango and Ngoyo whose port of Malemba was a busy Atlantic slave-trading harbour.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kakongo was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kongo-language">Kikongo</a>-speaking kingdoms north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo</a> estuary that recognised the senior <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-loango">Kingdom of Loango</a> in early times. By the mid-16th century it had its own king, ruling from Kinguele, with lands between the coast and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayombe">Mayombe</a> forest.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its sacred king was elected by senior lineages and surrounded by ritual restrictions, and regional chiefs controlled much of the land. In the 18th century the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malembo">Malemba</a> became a major market for French and English slavers, and the Abbe Proyart's history of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-loango">Loango</a> and Kakongo (1776), based on French missionaries' reports, described the court and customs.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> Kakongo declined into a cluster of chiefdoms. In 1885 its lands were divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-angola">Portuguese Angola</a>, where they formed part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-congo">Cabinda</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kakongo.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kakongo" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Dekodrak · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Banner_of_the_Kingdom_of_Kakongo_(c._1883).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1550 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kinguele</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Kongo religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kikongo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Angola, Democratic Republic of the Congo</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kakongo</dd></dl></aside></div>
